---
title: Separator
---

# Separator

A thin rule dividing content. Basecoat ships no component — it is the
`border` token applied as a border (horizontal `<hr>`) or a
one-pixel-wide block (vertical), written inline.

## Horizontal

An `<hr>` carries the separator role natively — no attributes to add.

<%= embed :separator, :horizontal %>

```html
<p>Browse the patient record.</p>
<hr class="border-0 border-t border-border">
<p>Recent observations are listed below.</p>
```

## Vertical

A one-pixel block inside a flex row; `self-stretch` makes it follow
the row's height. A `<div>` has no separator semantics of its own, so
add `role="separator"` and `aria-orientation="vertical"` (the role
defaults to horizontal).

<%= embed :separator, :vertical %>

```html
<div class="flex h-8 items-center gap-4">
  Docs
  <div class="w-px self-stretch bg-border" role="separator"
       aria-orientation="vertical"></div>
  Source
  <div class="w-px self-stretch bg-border" role="separator"
       aria-orientation="vertical"></div>
  API
</div>
```

## When to use

- Dividing groups inside one surface where a heading would be too
  heavy: menu sections, footer link groups, toolbar clusters
  (vertical).

## When not to use

- Between cards or page sections — the layout gap already separates
  them (see *Spacing and layout*); a rule on top is double
  punctuation.
- As decoration under headings — the heading is the separator.
- When the two sides deserve names — use headings, not a bare line.

## Do and don't

- Do prefer whitespace first; reach for a separator only when
  proximity alone fails to group.
- Do use `self-stretch` for the vertical form inside flex rows — not
  a styled border on a neighbour.
- Don't stack separators to create spacing — spacing comes from
  `gap-*`.
- Don't recolour separators; `border` token only, or the page grows
  a second visual language.

## Accessibility and content

- `<hr>` is announced as a boundary natively; purely decorative rules
  can add `aria-hidden="true"` to stay silent.
- The vertical `<div>` form needs `role="separator"` and
  `aria-orientation="vertical"` to be announced the same way.
- It has no text; if you want to write on a divider, the content
  wants a heading instead.
